---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Loop from '@/components/motion/scenes/Loop.astro';
import { LoopControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/LoopControls';
import { MotionLoopReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-loop.tsx';
import { MotionLoopAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-loop.ts';
import MotionLoopSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-loop.svelte';

const defineSpin = `import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

// A spinner that turns once a second. pause: 'control' says the page offers a button that holds it.
defineMotion('spin', {
  verb: 'loop',
  keyframes: [{ rotate: 0 }, { rotate: 360 }],
  pause: 'control',
});`;

const defineIndeterminate = `import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

// The bar is the progress itself, so the loop is essential: a paused bar would look finished.
defineMotion('indeterminate', {
  verb: 'loop',
  keyframes: [{ x: -140 }, { x: 340 }],
  pause: 'essential',
  reason: 'The bar shows that work is running; a paused bar would look finished.',
});`;

const pauseFromScript = `import { getMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

const spinner = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#spin')!;

// pause() holds the loop on its current frame, and play() resumes it, clearing the pause.
getMotion(spinner, 'loop')?.pause();
getMotion(spinner, 'loop')?.play();`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="loop"
  title="Motion: loop, and pause it"
  description="Loop a motion while its element is in view, and pause it from the page: a loop states how it is paused. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
>
  <ForFramework as="span" slot="summary">
    <Fragment slot="html">Put <code>data-motion</code> on an element: it loops forever while it is in view, and holds still when you set <code>data-motion-paused</code>.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">Render an element as a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> with the name of a loop: it loops forever while it is in view, and holds still when you pass <code>paused</code>.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotion</code> on an element: it loops forever while it is in view, and holds still while <code>[udxMotionPaused]</code> is true.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">Attach <code>motion()</code> to an element: it loops forever while it is in view, and holds still while the <code>paused</code> option is true.</Fragment>
  </ForFramework>

  <MotionStage scene="loop" label="Live example: Loop, and pause it" height={[240, 408]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><LoopControls client:visible /></Fragment>
    <Loop size="page" />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>Loop an element while it is in view</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>Put <code>data-motion</code> on the element with the name of a loop, such as <code>rotate</code>, and call <code>connectMotion()</code> once for the page.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>Render the element as a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> with the name of a loop, such as <code>name="rotate"</code>.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>Add <code>Motion</code> to your component's <code>imports</code>, and put <code>udxMotion</code> on the element with the name of a loop, such as <code>udxMotion="rotate"</code>.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>Import <code>motion</code> from <code>@udixio/ui-svelte</code>, and attach it to the element with <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('rotate')&#125;</code>.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    A loop plays its keyframes forever, from the moment it is bound. It pauses by itself while its element is out of view or
    its tab is hidden, and plays again when both hold.
  </p>
  <p>
    When its element sits in a box that clips it, with <code>overflow: hidden</code> or <code>clip</code>, the loop watches that
    box instead. A marquee that slides out of its clipping box keeps playing while the box is in view.
  </p>
  <p>The presets <code>rotate</code> (a full turn each second), <code>breathe</code> (swells to 104 % and back) and <code>blink</code> (one dim flash each second) are loops.</p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-loop" previewHeight={[120, 112]}>
    <MotionLoopReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionLoopAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionLoopSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2>Define your own loop</h2>
  <p>
    Define a motion with the verb <code>loop</code>, and give it keyframes. Its timing is a curve, and its <code>pause</code>
    says how a visitor can stop it. Every loop states how it is paused, because WCAG 2.2.2 asks that moving, blinking or
    scrolling content which starts by itself, lasts more than five seconds and runs beside other content can be paused,
    stopped or hidden, unless it is essential.
  </p>
  <Code data-code={defineSpin} data-language="ts" />
  <ul>
    <li><code>pause: 'control'</code>: the page offers a visible control that pauses the loop. Use it for decoration, as the presets do.</li>
    <li><code>pause: 'essential'</code> with a <code>reason</code>: the loop is the content itself, such as an indeterminate progress bar. The validator requires the reason, and refuses it on a <code>control</code> loop.</li>
  </ul>
  <Code data-code={defineIndeterminate} data-language="ts" />

  <h2>Pause the loop from the page</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>Set <code>data-motion-paused</code> on the element to hold the loop, and remove the attribute to play it again. <code>connectMotion()</code> watches the attribute.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>Pass <code>paused</code> to the <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>, or to <code>useMotion</code>: <code>true</code> holds the loop, and <code>false</code> plays it again. The example's button sets it from a state.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>Bind <code>[udxMotionPaused]</code> to a boolean: <code>true</code> holds the loop, and <code>false</code> plays it again.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>Return <code>paused</code> from the options of <code>motion()</code>: <code>true</code> holds the loop, and <code>false</code> plays it again.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>From script, in any framework, <code>getMotion(element, 'loop')</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>, returns the binding of the loop. Its <code>pause()</code> holds the loop, and its <code>play()</code> resumes it and clears the pause.</p>
  <Code data-code={pauseFromScript} data-language="ts" />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>A loop takes a curve as its timing, and states how it is paused with <code>pause</code>. A <code>reason</code> is needed with an essential loop.</p>
  <MotionOptions
    verb="loop"
    overrides={{
      timing: {
        values: '{ duration, easing }',
        default: 'duration.extraLong4, easing.linear',
        description: 'A curve, { duration, easing }: a loop has no spring. Without it, a loop runs for one second, linearly.',
      },
      pause: {
        description: 'control: the page offers a visible control that pauses the loop. essential: the loop is the content itself, and the page offers none. Every loop states one.',
      },
    }}
  />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>
    Without a <code>timing</code>, a loop runs for one second, linearly. <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> scales its duration and
    the distances of its keyframes, and <code>0</code> stops it.
  </p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['duration.extraLong4', 'easing.linear']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    Under reduced motion, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code>, a loop does not play: the element keeps its stylesheet state.
    When reduced motion is turned off, the loop starts again, paused if the page has paused it. See
    <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you define a loop with <code>pause: 'control'</code>, add a visible control that calls <code>pause()</code> on it, or sets <code>data-motion-paused</code>. Without one, the visitor cannot stop the loop.</li>
    <li>If something else moves by itself, such as a carousel that advances, check it with <code>watchActive</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion/active</code>. It reports when the element is in view, in a shown tab, and with motion not reduced.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
